  "description": "Shared vault-relative path validity vectors (SPEC.md section 6.2). Both \"/\" and \"\\\" are separators. A path is valid only if it does not start with a separator (which also rules out UNC, \\\\?\\ and \\\\.\\ prefixes), does not start with a drive letter followed by a colon (C:, C:x, C:\\x, C:/x), and has no segment that is empty or made only of dots and spaces (., .., and forms such as '.. ' or '...' that Windows normalizes to them). Names that are merely unmaterializable on Windows (reserved device names, colons elsewhere, trailing dots or spaces) are valid. Paths that pass are safe to touch the disk with (every path received from the server must). \"new\" is the stricter rule for a path a commit may create or rename a document into (decision, issue 191): valid, containing no \"\\\" at all, so that a/b and a\\b can never both be live, and not starting with any single code point followed by a colon (1:x, \u00e9:x), which Windows also reads as a drive (decision, issue 148); a path that is valid but not new is accepted only as the unchanged path of an already-live document. Both internal/vaultpath (Go) and plugin/src/core/vaultpath (TypeScript) must return valid and new exactly as given for every vector. Look-alike separators (U+FF0F, U+FF3C, U+2215, U+29F5) and fullwidth full stops (U+FF0E) are ordinary characters, not separators or dots (decision, issue 148). Length and character-set limits are checked separately (testdata/paths/text.json).",
